.TH CHAT 1
.SH NAME
chat \- Fuse Chat terminal client for ChatGPT
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chat
.br
.B chat --probe
.br
.B chat --login-request FILE
.br
.B chat --import FILE
.br
.B chat --instructions FILE
.br
.B chat --ask TEXT
.br
.B chat --list
.br
.B chat --history
.br
.B chat --history-full
.br
.B chat --workspace DIR
.br
.B chat --workspace DIR --ask TEXT
.SH DESCRIPTION
Fuse Chat runs on both Riplix/RVM and native Fuse-X. It starts with empty,
independent local history. GPT-6.1 Sol Light uses model gpt-6.1-sol with
low reasoning effort. It does not import existing ChatGPT conversations.
.PP
DNS, TCP, certificate-verified TLS, token refresh, HTTP and streaming run
inside the guest. Inference is provided by OpenAI using your authorized
ChatGPT plan. A Mac helper completes initial browser sign-in. No API key
or Codex credential file is used.
.SH KEYS
Enter sends. Ctrl-O inserts a newline. Ctrl-N creates a conversation.
Tab switches between the composer and conversation list; arrows choose a
conversation and Enter opens it. F3 (or Ctrl-B) hides or shows the list and saves that
preference. Tab temporarily reveals a hidden list to choose a conversation.
By default, narrow terminals hide the list automatically; F3 can show it
explicitly. The minimum size is 32x14.
Page Up and Page Down scroll the reply.
F3 passes directly through mux; Ctrl-B requires the mux prefix twice.
F4 toggles compact/full tool details without discarding a streamed reply.
Ctrl-R retries an unanswered message. Escape cancels an active reply.
F2 imports /mnt/host/chat-login.json. F10 or Ctrl-D exits. Bracketed paste
inserts newlines without sending the pasted message.
.SH COMPOSER
Left/Right move by Unicode grapheme. Up/Down move through wrapped input rows.
Home/End or Ctrl-A/E move to the beginning/end of the current logical line.
Ctrl-W deletes the previous whitespace-delimited word. Alt-B/F move by word;
Alt-D deletes the next word and Alt-Backspace deletes the previous word.
Ctrl-U cuts everything before the cursor; Ctrl-K cuts to the end of the line
(or joins lines when already at the end). Ctrl-Y restores the last cut.
Ctrl-L redraws the screen. Alt shortcuts can also be entered as Escape then
the letter, with no pause. Bracketed paste does not execute these shortcuts.
.PP
In a workspace, type @ to browse files and folders. Filter by typing a prefix;
Up/Down select, Tab or Enter completes, and Escape dismisses the chooser
without stopping a running reply. Selecting a folder continues into that
directory. Paths containing spaces, quotes or backslashes are quoted and
escaped automatically. Enter accepts a completion before a subsequent Enter
sends the message. Emails are not treated as file mentions.
.PP
Relative references such as @docs/readme.txt resolve from the conversation's
workspace; absolute guest paths are also accepted. Dotfiles are shown when
the current name prefix starts with a dot. Completion scans one directory
level, with bounded results; it does not recursively crawl or attach contents.
The model receives the reference as text and reads the file with its tools
when needed. Plain chats do not provide workspace file completion.
.SH CUSTOM INSTRUCTIONS
$HOME/.chat/.instructions is an optional UTF-8 text dotfile (up to 16 KiB).
With CHAT_HOME, it is $CHAT_HOME/.instructions. Its contents are added to the
model instructions for every conversation, including workspace chats and
--ask. The file is read for each model request, so editing it takes effect
without restarting the app. Conversation histories are not rewritten.
.PP
chat --instructions FILE validates and copies a file into this private
location with mode 0600. Edit the dotfile directly with any guest editor,
or import an updated copy. Empty or remove it to disable custom instructions.
An unreadable, invalid UTF-8, binary or oversized file stops the request with
an error instead of silently dropping preferences. No personal instructions
are built into the executable or shipped in the source tree.
.SH WORKSPACES AND TOOLS
Enter /workspace DIR in the composer to create a workspace conversation,
or launch chat --workspace DIR. The directory must exist. Its absolute guest
path is saved with that conversation and shown above the messages. Reopening
or selecting the conversation restores its workspace. /chat or Ctrl-N creates
a plain chat without tools.
.PP
Workspace conversations let the assistant list directories, read UTF-8 files,
write files, replace one exact text occurrence, and run commands through the
guest's /bin/sh. Calls and results appear in the transcript and are replayed
to the model until it produces its answer. Calls run automatically in the
workspace using the guest user's permissions. The workspace is a working
directory, not a filesystem sandbox: absolute paths and shell commands can
access anything permitted to that guest user, including writable Mac mounts.
Plain chats do not advertise or execute tools.
.PP
The transcript shows a compact operation/path or command, result status and
a preview of at most 192 octets/three lines. Long file reads and generated
source are folded. F4 expands or collapses tool details. Full results remain
in the saved history and model context; folding only changes their display.
--history prints the compact view; --history-full includes full tool details
within the existing 128 KiB display window.
.PP
Commands have /dev/null stdin, merged stdout/stderr, an exit status, and a
1-600 second deadline selected by the assistant. Output is limited to 32 KiB
per command. Background descendants are stopped on completion, timeout or
Escape; persistent interactive sessions are not supported. File reads are
paged by guest octet offset with a 32 KiB maximum; file writes and exact edits
are limited to 64 KiB and preserve existing regular-file permissions. Writes
use a same-directory temporary and rename; final symlinks are refused.
.PP
Escape stops the turn and its command group. Effects already performed are
not rolled back. Calls are saved before execution and results afterward.
An interrupted call without a saved result receives an explicit interrupted
receipt when the conversation is reopened; it is never automatically run
again. Ctrl-R continues an unfinished tool turn from saved results. A turn
stops after 32 tool rounds; a conversation supports 512 call records within
the existing 4 MiB history limit. Start a new workspace conversation as needed.
.PP
The tools use installed guest programs. Guest cc currently emits Ripple RXE
executables on both platforms: Riplix/RVM can execute those, while Fuse-X still
needs the Mac-side compiler for native FXE output. Shell scripts and existing
guest interpreters/programs run on either platform. No Mac command bridge or
additional compiler backend is introduced by chat.
.SH SIGN IN ON FUSE-X
In the guest:
.nf
chat --login-request /mnt/host/chat-request.json
.fi
On the Mac, from the repository root:
.nf
python3 ports/chat/login.py \
  --request fuse-x-shared/chat-request.json \
  --output fuse-x-shared/chat-login.json
.fi
Complete Continue with ChatGPT in the browser, then in the guest:
.nf
chat --import /mnt/host/chat-login.json
chat
.fi
Delete the transfer file on the Mac after successful import. The guest owns
refresh from then on. Do not import the same rotating refresh token into
several guests. Each guest has its own persisted host ID and registration.
.SH SIGN IN ON RVM
Run chat once, exit, then shut down the guest. On the Mac:
.nf
python3 ports/chat/disk.py request build/fuzix/fuzix.img /tmp/chat-request.json
python3 ports/chat/login.py --request /tmp/chat-request.json --output /tmp/chat-login.json
python3 ports/chat/disk.py credentials build/fuzix/fuzix.img /tmp/chat-login.json
.fi
Substitute the actual guest disk path. disk.py recognizes both RVM FAT-wrapped
and Fuse-X raw C16 disks and refuses a locked, open or dirty image. Boot and run chat.
Delete the transfer file after verifying sign-in. --state selects a nondefault
guest history directory for disk transfers.
.SH STORAGE
$HOME/.chat contains mode-0600 credentials and conversation JSONL files in a
mode-0700 directory. CHAT_HOME overrides that directory. A per-directory lock
prevents simultaneous writers and refresh-token rotation races.
.PP
Full assistant output items, phases and encrypted reasoning are replayed on
subsequent turns. Requests set store=false. Only a completed response received
over a completely framed HTTP message is committed. Failed, incomplete or
cancelled replies preserve the user's message for explicit retry.
.PP
Completed output-item events are retained when the final response snapshot
omits its messages. If necessary, confirmed-complete streamed text is saved
as an assistant message. Empty responses report an error rather than success.
.PP
The first release supports 128 conversations, 4 MiB per conversation, an 8 KiB
composer, 128 KiB display/reply text and 512 KiB streaming events with 4096 JSON
tokens per event. Context-limit errors require a new conversation; no silent
summarization or model fallback occurs. Replies render as wrapped plain text.
Attachments and chat deletion are not included.
.SH OPTIONS
--probe tests public OpenAI HTTPS without credentials or inference charges.
--ask sends TEXT in the latest conversation and streams to stdout. --list
lists stored conversations; --history prints the latest conversation's text.
An empty --ask argument retries an unanswered last message.
.PP
--instructions FILE installs custom instructions for all chats. The chat-list
visibility preference is saved separately as $CHAT_HOME/.sidebar (or
$HOME/.chat/.sidebar).
.PP
--login-request writes a public host-ID request. --import validates the
registration's host ID and required scopes and copies credentials into private
storage. The input file must be owned by the guest user and mode 0600.
.PP
login.py --reauthorize FILE reuses this app's existing registration for the
same guest/account. Never supply another application's credentials. Reexport
this guest's current account.json first; an old transfer copy can hold a
previously rotated refresh token. Sign-in and inference availability depend
on the ChatGPT account and workspace permissions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
CHAT_API_ORIGIN and CHAT_AUTH_ORIGIN override HTTPS origins for development
fixtures. CHAT_CA overrides /etc/ssl/cert.pem. Plain HTTP, redirects and
certificate-verification bypasses are not supported. The browser callback uses
HTTP only on the Mac's loopback interface for OAuth.
.SH IMPLEMENTATION
ports/chat contains the shared C16 client, build driver, browser sign-in helper
and independent tests. The UI uses the existing guest Unicode/curses library;
TLS uses the existing guest wolfSSL library. A forked guest worker performs
network requests while the terminal remains responsive.
